1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:03,280
What happens to an AI
agent when the process

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:07,840
executing it disappears? Hello,
I'm Russell and welcome to

3
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:11,560
episode 12 of the Articulate
Journal, where I'm exploring

4
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:15,410
what it takes to build an
AI-native architecture from

5
00:00:15,410 --> 00:00:18,940
first principles. In the
previous episode, I explored what

6
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:21,480
AI systems mean when
they talk about memory.

7
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:25,450
And what initially looked like
one architectural concept turned

8
00:00:25,530 --> 00:00:27,840
out to be several
different responsibilities.

9
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:32,470
Knowledge belongs to the Knowledge
Model, conversation has its

10
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:36,740
own state. Embeddings and
indexes support retrieval and can

11
00:00:36,810 --> 00:00:40,990
usually be rebuilt. Even
much of the temporary context

12
00:00:41,070 --> 00:00:43,990
used while an agent is
reasoning can potentially be

13
00:00:44,070 --> 00:00:47,680
reconstructed. But one form
of state was different.

14
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:51,960
Execution. If an agent is
halfway through some work

15
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:54,950
and a process disappears,
how does the system know

16
00:00:55,030 --> 00:00:59,680
where it got to? At first,
the requirement seems

17
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:02,890
fairly simple. If an agent
is interrupted, I want

18
00:01:02,970 --> 00:01:05,570
it to continue. Maybe it's
analysing the impact of

19
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:09,760
an architectural change. Maybe
it needs clarification from me.

20
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:14,810
Maybe another agent needs to
contribute, or perhaps it's

21
00:01:14,890 --> 00:01:19,160
waiting for approval. Whatever
the reason, I don't want

22
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:22,040
the lifetime of that
work tied to the lifetime

23
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:25,120
of a thread, a container,
or a particular agent

24
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:30,140
instance. If that process disappears,
the work shouldn't disappear

25
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:33,770
with it. But that raises another question.

26
00:01:34,130 --> 00:01:37,020
Do I actually need to preserve execution?

27
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:39,740
Couldn't I just run the agent again?

28
00:01:40,210 --> 00:01:44,540
Imagine an agent analysing
the impact of a proposed

29
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:49,880
architectural change. It retrieves
relevant architectural knowledge.

30
00:01:50,010 --> 00:01:52,710
It identifies the affected capabilities.

31
00:01:53,110 --> 00:01:56,090
It looks at decisions and
principles that might be

32
00:01:56,170 --> 00:01:59,790
relevant. Then it asks me a
question before continuing.

33
00:02:00,070 --> 00:02:02,850
If that process disappears at
that point, perhaps nothing

34
00:02:02,930 --> 00:02:04,840
particularly important has been lost.

35
00:02:05,210 --> 00:02:10,080
The Knowledge Model still exists,
conversation exists, the context

36
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:14,080
can be reconstructed. The agent
can retrieve the information

37
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:17,510
again and continue reasoning.
It might perform some work

38
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:21,980
twice. Perhaps that's acceptable.
So maybe the execution state

39
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:24,790
isn't particularly special after all.

40
00:02:25,210 --> 00:02:28,170
The problem appears when
the agent starts to do

41
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:31,680
things. Let's change
the scenario slightly.

42
00:02:32,210 --> 00:02:35,930
Before asking me for
approval, the agent records a

43
00:02:36,010 --> 00:02:40,720
proposed architectural decision. It
invokes another capability, sends a

44
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:44,470
notification asking me to
review something, and then it

45
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:49,650
waits. Now imagine the process disappears.

46
00:02:49,950 --> 00:02:50,750
When it starts again, we
have a slightly different

47
00:02:50,827 --> 00:02:54,780
problem. Was the proposed
decision already recorded?

48
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:56,900
Did the other capability complete?

49
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:58,900
Was the notification already sent?

50
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:02,680
If I simply restart the
reasoning process, should those

51
00:03:02,690 --> 00:03:06,790
actions happen again? Suddenly
the problem isn't remembering what

52
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:09,520
the agent knew. The
problem is knowing what the

53
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:13,710
system has already done. And
that distinction matters.

54
00:03:14,210 --> 00:03:17,460
This is where execution
begins to look different from

55
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:20,847
the other forms of state I explored in the

56
00:03:20,927 --> 00:03:26,130
previous episode. Knowledge can
be retrieved, conversations can be

57
00:03:26,210 --> 00:03:30,480
replayed. Context can be
reconstructed, and reasoning can often

58
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:33,560
be performed again. But
once work has caused an

59
00:03:33,610 --> 00:03:36,960
effect outside the reasoning
process, we need to know

60
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:39,860
whether that effect has actually happened.

61
00:03:40,140 --> 00:03:42,910
The runtime needs to know
what completed, what failed,

62
00:03:43,010 --> 00:03:46,360
which tools have already been
executed, which external effects

63
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:49,680
have occurred. Execution state
isn't simply another form of memory.

64
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:51,830
It's a record of progress.

65
00:03:52,210 --> 00:03:56,090
This becomes particularly important
because agents don't operate in

66
00:03:56,170 --> 00:04:00,850
isolation. They call tools.
They invoke other capabilities.

67
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:03,790
They update systems.
They request approvals.

68
00:04:04,210 --> 00:04:07,790
They communicate with external services.

69
00:04:08,210 --> 00:04:11,950
Every time execution crosses one
of those boundaries, something

70
00:04:12,030 --> 00:04:14,840
may happen that can't simply
be forgotten or performed

71
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:19,680
again without consequence. Sending
the same notification twice might

72
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:23,680
only be annoying. Creating
the same record twice could

73
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:27,290
be more serious. Invoking another
capability twice could cause

74
00:04:27,370 --> 00:04:29,790
an entire different chain
of work to happen again.

75
00:04:30,090 --> 00:04:33,460
So recovering an agent isn't
simply about reconstructing its

76
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:35,740
prompt and asking the model to continue.

77
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:40,020
The system needs to understand
what has already happened

78
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:43,830
before it can safely decide
what should happen next.

79
00:04:44,210 --> 00:04:47,750
And then we introduce us, human beings.

80
00:04:48,210 --> 00:04:50,680
Suppose an agent reaches
a point where it needs

81
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:52,880
an architect to approve something.

82
00:04:53,170 --> 00:04:55,680
The response might arrive
five minutes later.

83
00:04:56,010 --> 00:04:59,120
It might arrive tomorrow.
It might arrive next week.

84
00:04:59,210 --> 00:05:01,680
And it might never arrive at all.

85
00:05:02,210 --> 00:05:05,818
Keeping a process alive
while it waits isn't really

86
00:05:05,898 --> 00:05:10,010
the answer. The execution needs
to survive independently of

87
00:05:10,010 --> 00:05:12,770
the process currently performing the work.

88
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:16,310
But there's another complication.
The world may have changed

89
00:05:16,390 --> 00:05:17,880
while the agent was waiting.

90
00:05:18,210 --> 00:05:21,840
New architectural knowledge
may have been accepted.

91
00:05:22,210 --> 00:05:23,920
Another decision may have been made.

92
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:26,840
The proposal itself may
have been superseded.

93
00:05:27,190 --> 00:05:30,000
The state the agent originally
reasoned over may no

94
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,120
longer represent the current architecture.

95
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:37,130
So, resuming execution isn't
necessarily the same as waking

96
00:05:37,210 --> 00:05:41,150
up a suspended process. We've
re-entered a system that

97
00:05:41,170 --> 00:05:43,780
may have changed while we were away.

98
00:05:44,210 --> 00:05:47,020
Time has become part of the architecture.

99
00:05:48,190 --> 00:05:51,150
And this is where the
understanding of the problem

100
00:05:51,230 --> 00:05:56,310
changed. Durable execution isn't
really about keeping the agent

101
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:59,690
alive. It's about preserving
the continuity of work.

102
00:06:00,110 --> 00:06:03,960
We don't necessarily need to
preserve every temporary thought

103
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:06,790
or every piece of reasoning context.

104
00:06:07,210 --> 00:06:09,680
Much of that can be reconstructed.

105
00:06:10,210 --> 00:06:12,350
What we need to preserve is enough of the

106
00:06:12,430 --> 00:06:16,480
execution to understand what
work is completed, what work

107
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:19,850
remains, what effects
have already occurred.

108
00:06:20,210 --> 00:06:26,130
What we're waiting for
and what can safely happen

109
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:29,870
next. The process performing
the work may disappear.

110
00:06:30,010 --> 00:06:34,250
The execution should not.
The process is temporary.

111
00:06:34,430 --> 00:06:38,080
The execution is durable.
This also gives me a

112
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:41,120
much cleaner boundary
around the runtime itself.

113
00:06:41,210 --> 00:06:47,910
In the previous episode I
discovered that the runtime

114
00:06:47,990 --> 00:06:51,770
owns far less memory than
I initially expected.

115
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:54,680
It doesn't own architectural knowledge.

116
00:06:55,010 --> 00:06:56,940
That's the responsibility
of the Knowledge Model.

117
00:06:57,110 --> 00:06:59,680
It doesn't need to own every conversation.

118
00:07:00,010 --> 00:07:02,960
It doesn't need to treat
embeddings, indexes and caches

119
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:05,790
as permanent memory. Much
of that context required by

120
00:07:05,870 --> 00:07:09,120
an agent can be reconstructed
from the wider platform.

121
00:07:09,210 --> 00:07:11,680
But the execution is different.

122
00:07:12,210 --> 00:07:18,430
The runtime coordinates
work. If that work can span

123
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:24,240
processes, failures, agents, external
systems, humans and potentially days

124
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:27,290
of elapsed time, then preserving
the continuity of that

125
00:07:27,370 --> 00:07:30,890
execution becomes one of the
most important responsibilities.

126
00:07:31,210 --> 00:07:35,770
That gives me another important
requirement for ADR 0001.

127
00:07:36,170 --> 00:07:39,970
The runtime Articulate eventually
uses, needs to support work

128
00:07:40,050 --> 00:07:44,680
that survives interruption. It
needs pause and resume semantics.

129
00:07:45,010 --> 00:07:47,830
It needs to recover from failure,
support long-running execution

130
00:07:47,910 --> 00:07:50,860
and support humans becoming
part of that execution.

131
00:07:51,110 --> 00:07:54,750
It needs enough execution
history to understand what has

132
00:07:54,780 --> 00:07:59,310
already happened. And it
needs to coordinate work, and

133
00:07:59,390 --> 00:08:02,600
it needs to coordinate work
that may be distributed

134
00:08:02,650 --> 00:08:05,870
across different processes
and different points in time.

135
00:08:06,210 --> 00:08:09,840
Those aren't simply useful
runtime features anymore.

136
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:12,790
They've become the
architectural requirements.

137
00:08:13,210 --> 00:08:16,240
Over the last three episodes,
the question has gradually

138
00:08:16,310 --> 00:08:20,225
changed. I started asking which
agent runtime Articulate should

139
00:08:20,305 --> 00:08:23,430
use. Then I had to
understand what state the

140
00:08:23,510 --> 00:08:26,240
runtime actually owns. And
now I've discovered one of

141
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:29,840
the most important responsibilities
isn't really memory at all.

142
00:08:30,130 --> 00:08:33,840
It's preserving work across
failure and across time.

143
00:08:34,210 --> 00:08:37,940
Once tools have executed, once
other capabilities become involved,

144
00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:41,320
once humans enter the process
and once execution can

145
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:45,240
last longer than the process
performing it, simply starting

146
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:47,950
again isn't enough. We need continuity.

147
00:08:48,110 --> 00:08:50,380
We need to know what happened, and we need

148
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:51,940
to know what can safely happen again.

149
00:08:52,210 --> 00:08:55,920
That gives me the final
architectural requirement I need

150
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,240
for ADR 0001. Now I can go back to

151
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:02,180
the candidate runtimes and evaluate
them against the architecture

152
00:09:02,260 --> 00:09:06,090
Articulate actually needs.
And that's what we'll do for

153
00:09:06,170 --> 00:09:08,810
the next episode. See you on the next one.
